## Decoding the question
The question quotes a sceptical voice — "Is the quota **showing results**?" — and then asks you to "**rethink and discuss**" how quotas can lead to **political mainstreaming**. So: (1) explain the **17% quota**; (2) **judge** its results with evidence; (3) admit its **limits**; (4) propose **reforms** that turn the quota into mainstreaming. "Mainstreaming" means women becoming normal, equal actors in all of politics, not a separate group.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
A **gender quota** reserves a minimum share of seats or candidacies for women. **Political mainstreaming** means that women take part in **all** areas of politics — general seats, cabinets, party leadership, every policy field — as equals, not as a reserved minority.

#### ii) The argument
Pakistan's quota **is showing results**: it has raised women's numbers, produced landmark laws and changed the agenda. But it has not yet produced **mainstreaming**, because reserved-seat women depend on party leaders and few women win general seats. A **rethought** quota — directly elected, combined with strong candidate quotas and party reform — can make women part of the mainstream.

### 2. The quota explained
The **Legal Framework Order 2002** (validated by the **17th Amendment**, ==2003==) enlarged the National Assembly to **342** seats and reserved **60** for women — about **17%** — plus **17** Senate seats and **128** provincial seats. These are filled by **proportional representation** from party lists, each party receiving women's seats in proportion to the general seats it wins in each province. After the ==2018== merger of the tribal areas, the Assembly has **336** seats, still with **60** for women.

### 3. What mainstreaming requires
Mainstreaming would mean women winning **general seats**, holding **major ministries**, leading **parties** and shaping **all** policy — finance and security as well as social welfare. The test of a quota is whether it leads there, or keeps women in a separate box. The concept echoes **gender mainstreaming**, adopted at the **Beijing** conference (==1995==): gender should be part of all decisions, not a side issue handled by a women's ministry.

### 4. Results 1: numbers
In the ==1990s==, when reserved seats had lapsed, only **two to six** women sat in the National Assembly. Since ==2002==, women have held about **one-fifth** of seats. The **Devolution Plan** (==2000–01==) added about **36,000** women councillors. This is the "**fast track**" that **Drude Dahlerup** described — a jump to near-critical mass within a single election, which decades of gradual change had failed to produce.

### 5. Results 2: laws
The cross-party **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==) turned numbers into legislation:

- the **workplace harassment** law and Section 509 PPC (==2010==);
- the **Acid Control** and **Prevention of Anti-Women Practices** laws (==2011==), both private members' bills by women;
- the **NCSW Act** (==2012==);
- the **anti-honour-killing** and **anti-rape** laws (==2016==);
- provincial **domestic-violence** laws (==2013–2021==).

### 6. Results 3: agenda, activity and symbols

#### i) Agenda
Violence, harassment and inheritance became normal business of parliament, discussed in debates, questions and standing committees rather than dismissed as private family matters.

#### ii) Activity
Monitoring by **FAFEN** and **PILDAT** found women members among the most active in questions, resolutions and bills, with good attendance — evidence against the claim that they are passive tokens.

#### iii) Symbols
Women legislators, a woman Speaker (**Fahmida Mirza**, ==2008==) and a woman chief minister (**Maryam Nawaz**, ==2024==) have changed ideas of who can govern.

::: fig grid | The results of Pakistan's quota — and its limits.
@cols 2
Results: women's share up from under 3% to about one-fifth
Results: landmark laws of 2010–2016 driven by the caucus
Limits: reserved-seat women depend on party leaders
Limits: only 11 women won general NA seats in 2024
:::

### 7. Limits 1: party dependence and no constituency
Reserved seats are filled from **party lists**, so women owe their seats to party leaders and rarely defy them. They have **no constituency**, so they lack a power base, development funds and a route to general seats. Few reserved-seat women have later won a general seat, so the quota rarely acts as a stepping stone. The stigma of being "**nominated**" persists. The ==2024–25== legal dispute over whether PTI-backed independents should receive a share of reserved seats showed that these seats depend wholly on **party arithmetic**, not on women's own standing with voters.

### 8. Limits 2: few general seats; quota as a ceiling
Only **11** women won general National Assembly seats in ==2024== — about **4%** of 266. Parties treat reserved seats as women's **share** and give men nearly all winnable tickets. The **5%** ticket rule of the **Elections Act 2017** is weak, and women are often placed in **losing** constituencies.

### 9. Limits 3: marginal posts and elite capture
Women ministers usually receive "soft" portfolios such as women's development or social welfare, rarely finance, interior or foreign affairs; in ==2025== there were **none** at federal level. Many list places go to relatives of party leaders, so poor, rural and minority women remain unrepresented. Local quotas were also cut after ==2013== under new provincial laws, weakening the pipeline that the 2001 reform had created.

### 10. Rethink 1: direct election and constituencies
1. **Rotating reserved constituencies**, as in India's panchayats, would let voters elect women directly.
2. **Dual-member constituencies** with one woman member are another option.
3. Either would give women a **constituency**, legitimacy and independence from party leaders.
4. Reserved-seat members could at least be assigned **districts** to serve, with equal development funds, as an interim step.

### 11. Rethink 2: candidate quotas, party reform, pipeline and resources

#### i) Stronger candidate quotas
Raise the **5%** rule (towards **33%**) and require women in **winnable** seats, with sanctions.

#### ii) Party reform
Quotas in party executives and ticket committees; transparent list selection through internal party elections, so that list places reward work rather than family ties.

#### iii) Local pipeline
Restore **33%** women's seats in local government as a training ground, and encourage successful councillors to contest provincial and national general seats.

#### iv) Equal resources and roles
Equal development funds, offices, committee chairs and cabinet posts, including major ministries.

#### v) Lessons from Nepal
Nepal's 2015 constitution and **PR lists** produced about **35%** women in ==2026==; India's rotating panchayat seats have brought over a million women into directly elected local office. Both show that design, not culture, determines results.

### 12. Conclusion
The sceptics are only half right. Pakistan's quota **has shown results** — more women, landmark laws and a changed agenda. What it has not done is **mainstream** women into general seats, cabinets and party leadership. Rethinking its design — **direct election**, strong candidate quotas, party democracy and equal resources — would turn a reserved minority into equal participants.

## Why this answer scores
- Explains the **17% quota** accurately (60 of 342, LFO 2002).
- Judges results in **several areas** with evidence, and admits **limits**.
- Answers "**rethink**" with concrete design reforms and a regional comparison.
- A **grid figure** sets results against limits.
